WEBVTT

00:00.451 --> 00:03.201
(dramatic music)

00:08.010 --> 00:10.340
- [Narrator] Seasons change even on Mars.

00:10.340 --> 00:13.240
A look at how NASA's
Explorer study the weather

00:13.240 --> 00:14.980
and cope with change,

00:14.980 --> 00:18.370
especially in the case of
the Ingenuity helicopter,

00:18.370 --> 00:21.453
which now has to fly in
a thinner atmosphere.

00:22.810 --> 00:25.940
This visualization, known as NASA's Eyes,

00:25.940 --> 00:28.300
shows where the agencies two rovers

00:28.300 --> 00:30.910
and lander are touring the planet.

00:30.910 --> 00:32.730
For most of them, measuring the weather

00:32.730 --> 00:34.713
is a key part of their job.

00:35.930 --> 00:38.000
It's early summer in Jezero crater

00:38.000 --> 00:39.730
where NASA's Perseverance rover

00:39.730 --> 00:41.950
and the Ingenuity helicopter are exploring

00:41.950 --> 00:43.720
the South Seitahr region.

00:43.720 --> 00:47.520
Perseverance uses its Mars
Environmental Dynamic Analyzer,

00:47.520 --> 00:49.530
or MEDA, to measure temperature,

00:49.530 --> 00:52.490
humidity, wind speed, and direction.

00:52.490 --> 00:54.250
These wind sensors also measure

00:54.250 --> 00:57.600
the amount and size of dust
particles in the atmosphere,

00:57.600 --> 01:00.030
helping scientists
understand the dust cycle

01:00.030 --> 01:02.420
and its impact on weather.

01:02.420 --> 01:04.910
MEDA also provides the
Ingenuity helicopter

01:04.910 --> 01:08.130
with critical pre-flight
weather forecasts.

01:08.130 --> 01:11.630
Recently, warmer temperatures
and a thinning atmosphere

01:11.630 --> 01:13.810
have made it more difficult
for the helicopter

01:13.810 --> 01:16.310
to generate enough lift to fly.

01:16.310 --> 01:18.030
Its rotors had to spin faster

01:18.030 --> 01:19.740
than they had in previous flights,

01:19.740 --> 01:23.430
roughly 2,700 revolutions a minute.

01:23.430 --> 01:24.920
In the Southern hemisphere,

01:24.920 --> 01:26.970
where the Curiosity rover is driving

01:26.970 --> 01:29.470
in Gale crater, it's early winter.

01:29.470 --> 01:31.900
The Rover Environmental
Monitoring Station,

01:31.900 --> 01:34.310
REMs, provides daily weather reports

01:34.310 --> 01:36.260
and takes regular dust surveys

01:36.260 --> 01:39.770
to measure seasonal changes over time.

01:39.770 --> 01:41.920
The InSight lander is focused on

01:41.920 --> 01:44.090
what's happening below ground.

01:44.090 --> 01:46.620
In September, it measured
one of the biggest,

01:46.620 --> 01:49.690
longest lasting Mars quakes ever detected.

01:49.690 --> 01:52.190
Seismic waves from the magnitude 4.2

01:52.190 --> 01:55.650
shook InSight for nearly
an hour and a half.

01:55.650 --> 01:57.500
Finally, the fleet of orbiters,

01:57.500 --> 02:00.480
including Odyssey, the Mars
Reconnaissance Orbiter,

02:00.480 --> 02:03.300
and MAVEN help scientists
understand the scope

02:03.300 --> 02:05.560
and scale of storms from above.

02:05.560 --> 02:08.540
MRO produces a daily global weather map

02:08.540 --> 02:11.460
and provides us with amazing images.

02:11.460 --> 02:14.450
If you're interested in
dunes on other worlds,

02:14.450 --> 02:16.672
check out these recent photos.

02:16.672 --> 02:18.130
(upbeat music)

02:18.130 --> 02:19.410
To get the latest updates,

02:19.410 --> 02:23.930
follow @nasajpl and
@nasamars on social media,

02:23.930 --> 02:26.480
or take a deeper dive
on the mission websites

02:26.480 --> 02:28.913
at mars.nasa.gov.
